WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.648
A Cassandra table holds 50 million payments.

2
00:00:05.648 --> 00:00:11.141
Support asks for merchant M-0042's payments from ten o'clock to ten fifteen today.

3
00:00:11.141 --> 00:00:14.624
How does it find them without reading anything else?

4
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:16.663
A Cassandra table is a map.

5
00:00:16.663 --> 00:00:19.916
Its key is the partition key, here the merchant.

6
00:00:19.916 --> 00:00:23.643
Each value is a partition: every row for that merchant.

7
00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:29.791
Cassandra hashes the partition key to pick the nodes that hold its partition, usually three.

8
00:00:29.791 --> 00:00:33.011
A query that names the merchant goes only to them.

9
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:39.292
Inside a partition, rows are sorted by the clustering key, here the time the payment was created.

10
00:00:39.292 --> 00:00:42.557
That's the order they sit in, in memory and on disk.

11
00:00:42.767 --> 00:00:46.277
So ten o'clock to ten fifteen is one contiguous slice.

12
00:00:46.277 --> 00:00:51.868
The node seeks to the first row at ten o'clock, and reads until it passes ten fifteen.

13
00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:55.393
But a merchant never stops taking payments.

14
00:00:55.393 --> 00:01:01.409
At 40,000 a day, this partition passes the advised 100,000 rows on day three.

15
00:01:01.409 --> 00:01:05.237
After a year it holds 14 million rows, over 2 GB.

16
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.853
A partition never splits across nodes.

17
00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:11.920
Its three replicas carry all of it, and it never stops growing.

18
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:13.542
The fix is a bucket.

19
00:01:13.542 --> 00:01:17.003
Make the partition key the merchant and the day.

20
00:01:17.003 --> 00:01:22.915
Every day starts a new partition, so none holds more than 40,000 rows, about 6 MB.

21
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.632
The ten o'clock query names the merchant and today: still one partition, one slice.

22
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.311
The price: a week of history is now seven partitions, read in parallel and merged.

23
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.976
A Cassandra table is a map of partitions, each a sorted list of rows.

24
00:01:38.976 --> 00:01:43.388
Pick a partition key that every query names, and that stops growing.
